ref 17592186045417] [17592186524939 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186524726 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186524939 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186524737 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186524939 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186524939 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186524939 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186524601 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186524939 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "e45e0444-e71c-54ae-afbd-b2d40b2407b2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186524943 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186524713 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186524943 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186524714 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186524943 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186524753 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186524943 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186524768 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186524943 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186524820 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186524943 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186524943 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186524943 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186524768 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186524943 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "e7f79f08-c364-55a5-b2d4-7a59020d9e10" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186524951 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186524463 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186524951 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045492 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186524951 #edb/ref 1077 "field-1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186524951 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186524463 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186524951 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "fc3af737-6c46-5c87-a738-6dffb7ab08df" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186524953 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186524537 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186524953 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186524568 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186524953 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186524602 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186524953 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186524694 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186524953 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186524752 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186524953 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186524953 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186524953 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186524752 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186524953 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "fe1cad92-38bd-52de-a7fd-ee07bf798a45" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186524970 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186524916 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186524970 #edb/ref 1069 "q-98373" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186524970 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186524970 #edb/ref 1071 "Which of the following numbers are integers?\n\nI. $-1$\n\nII. $0$\n\nIII. $1$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186524970 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "20ed3ba1-b1a8-58f4-bd28-e42bcc1fa66a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186524970 #edb/ref 1074 "The integers consist of all positive and negative whole numbers and zero.\n\n$$\n…,-3,\\,-2,\\,-1,\\,0,\\,1,\\,2,\\,3,…\n$$\n\n \n\nOf the given numbers, $-1,$ $0,$ and $1$ are all integers.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186524972 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186524951 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186524972 #edb/ref 1069 "q-181026" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186524972 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186524972 #edb/ref 1071 "$7\\cdot (-0.9)=${{field-1}}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186524972 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "27d1fbdb-2975-5b0f-9d8e-58c27611f8b1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186524972 #edb/ref 1074 "We have a product of a positive number $(7)$ and a negative number $(-0.9).$ So the product will be negative:\n\n$$\n7\\cdot (-0.9)=-\\,\\boxed{}\n$$\n\nNow, to compute the number that goes in the box, we multiply $7$ by $0.9$ without any signs: $7\\cdot 0.9=6.3.$ So, we get\n\n$$\n7\\cdot (-0.9)=-6.3.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186524990 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186524914 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186524990 #edb/ref 1069 "q-155030" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186524990 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186524990 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/95/95726fe45adfcf88fb3d2380779380fb236a5dd0a3bb332847130a8cb5251251.png)\n\nWhich of the diagrams above shows three collinear points?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186524990 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "5af7e394-12e7-5f3a-b066-84aff669a022" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186524990 #edb/ref 1074 "Three (or more) points are collinear if a straight line passes through all points.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our diagrams in turn.\n\n- Diagram I shows three collinear points. There is a straight line that passes through all three of these points.\n\n![](images/61/611d93edb119e6bdc60bc3492b72d2c08730ef946232e3c3189b7d04eab43c18.png)\n\n- Diagram II shows three points that are *not* collinear. We can't draw a straight line that passes through all three points.\n\n![](images/32/32bb229224aac8d0b8feda0160c00d7afa9aa42827f448834a173b926a9dd48b.png)\n\n \n\n- Diagram III shows three points that are *not* collinear. We can't draw a straight line that passes through all three points.\n\n![](images/38/3886d294defeb0a4e43c5612c981b6b392a23067c0ecb51ebab6c2dab154c218.png)\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186524993 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186524855 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186524993 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186524868 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186524993 #edb/ref 1069 "q-236393" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186524993 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186524993 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/e6/e612ac8882e5abbe9f5eff3fc9f6e1f9e5e5d5827f01d232532ca8378c508793.png)\n\nExpress the number of hexagons to the total number of shapes as a ratio in its simplest form.\n\n{{field-1}}\n$:$\n{{field-2}}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186524993 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "63fb796f-6e2e-5f84-a08a-5f5d5b996ee4" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186524993 #edb/ref 1074 "We count the number of hexagons. We see that there are $4$ hexagons.\n\nWe count the total number of shapes. We see that there are $8$ shapes in total.\n\nSo the ratio of hexagons to total shapes is $4:8.$\n\nWe can simplify this ratio by dividing both numbers in a ratio by $4:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\,\\,4 &  & : & \\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,8 &  \\\\ \\,\\,↓÷\\,\\,4 &  &  &  & \\,\\,\\,↓÷\\,\\,4 &  \\\\ \\,\\,1 &  & : &  & \\,\\,\\,2 & \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the ratio of hexagons to the total shapes is $1:2.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186525001 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186524928 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186525001 #edb/ref 1069 "q-75654" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186525001 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186525001 #edb/ref 1071 "$(-2)\\cdot (-8.6)=$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186525001 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "81ed1269-d411-55d0-9278-76332ca965f4" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186525001 #edb/ref 1074 "We have the product of two negative numbers, which gives a positive number.\n\n$$\n(-2)\\cdot (-8.6)=2\\cdot 8.6=17.2.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186525006 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186524911 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186525006 #edb/ref 1069 "q-108484" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186525006 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186525006 #edb/ref 1071 "Translate the following phrase into an algebraic expression: \"Eleven more than a number.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186525006 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "8a3d8f81-e9cc-5df6-a543-4a7637a4bf37" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186525006 #edb/ref 1074 "If we denote our number by $x$, then the phrase \"eleven more than a number\" can be written as\n\n$$\nx+11.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186525009 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186524953 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186525009 #edb/ref 1069 "q-61272" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186525009 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186525009 #edb/ref 1071 "What is the distance between the points $(0,-2)$  and $(0,-4)?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186525009 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "8f463b76-07fb-5ba1-955a-1c27fbeaf1d4" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186525009 #edb/ref 1074 "**Method 1** \n\nWe plot the given points in the coordinate plane.\n\n![](images/9e/9edd6fb0f5b8dbf861a10cb559e597abaee54d4f833735912ab8ecb8b6e6091a.png)\n\nThe distance between two points is the length of the line segment that connects them.\n\nFrom the diagram, we see that the length of the segment is $2$ units, so the distance equals $2.$\n\n![](images/ee/ee815ded554b01fd73cfd3577cfd7a21ea1360a32414de62a2f43f8b8612fd67.png)\n\n**Method 2**\n\nWe can find the distance between the two points by finding the distance of each point from the $y$-axis and then subtracting.\n\n![](images/19/19d94e1c26e61bfccdbab7083781ea5fb164910c54719135b348a60002c27a0b.png)\n\nThe point $(0,-2)$ is at a distance of $2$ from the $x$-axis, and the point $(0,-4)$ is at a distance of $4$ from the $x$-axis. Therefore, the distance between the two points is $4-2=2.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186525012 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186524923 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186525012 #edb/ref 1069 "q-61194" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186525012 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186525012 #edb/ref 1071 "In a particular year, firefighters in a city responded to $40$ emergencies. The following year, firefighters attended $165%$ of the total number of emergencies compared to the previous year. How many emergencies did firefighters attend in the second year?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186525012 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "90c33764-1c3e-51f1-880d-65fb7c77f172" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186525012 #edb/ref 1074 "To solve this problem, we can use the formula:\n\n$$\n\\text{part}=\\text{percentage}\\times \\text{whole}\n$$\n\nIn our case, the whole is the total number of emergencies that firefighters attended in the first year, which is $40$ emergencies. The percentage is $165%,$ and the part is the total number of emergencies firefighters attended in the second year.\n\n**Method 1**\n\nFirst, we convert the percentage to a decimal:\n\n$$\n165%=165÷100=1.65\n$$\n\nApplying our formula, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{part} & =\\text{percentage}\\times \\text{whole} \\\\  & =165%\\times 40 \\\\  & =1.65\\times 40 \\\\  & =66\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $165%$ of $40$ is $66.$ So, firefighters attended $66$ emergencies in the second year.\n\n**Method 2**\n\nFirst, we convert the percentage to a fraction:\n\n$$\n165%=\\frac{165}{100}=\\frac{165÷5}{100÷5}=\\frac{33}{20}\n$$\n\nApplying our formula, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{part} & =\\text{percentage}\\times \\text{whole} \\\\  & =165%\\times 40 \\\\  & =\\frac{33}{20}\\times 40 \\\\  & =33\\times \\frac{40}{20} \\\\  & =33\\times 2 \\\\  & =66\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $165%$ of $40$ is $66.$ So, firefighters attended $66$ emergencies in the second year." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186525035 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186524917 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186525035 #edb/ref 1069 "q-128106" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186525035 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186525035 #edb/ref 1071 "$201-249=$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186525035 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "bd178561-a381-5104-8041-a4f6b40537cb" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186525035 #edb/ref 1074 "Since $249$ is larger than $201,$ the answer will be negative.\n\nWe start by solving an easier problem:\n\n$$\n249-201\n$$\n\nNext, we carry out the subtraction:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} &  & \\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\, \\\\  &  & \\,\\,\\,2\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,4\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,9\\,\\,\\, \\\\ \\,\\,\\,\\,-\\,\\,\\,\\, &  & \\,\\,\\,2\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,0\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,1\\,\\,\\, \\\\  &  &  & \\,\\,\\,4\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,8\\,\\,\\,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n249-201=48.\n$$\n\nHowever, since our answer must be negative, we need to change the sign of the answer above. \n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n201-249=-48.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186525042 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186524943 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186525042 #edb/ref 1069 "q-60248" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186525042 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186525042 #edb/ref 1071 "From left to right, what are the missing numbers in the following sentence?\n\nIn a liquid, there are $\\frac{2}{3}$ quarts of honey for each quart of water. Therefore, the liquid has a ratio of $\\boxed{}$ quarts of honey to $\\boxed{}$ quarts of water." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186525042 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "ca5fdebc-3254-5299-89b6-95fbebb635d8" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186525042 #edb/ref 1074 "Let's rewrite a fraction $\\frac{2}{3}$ as a ratio:\n\n$$\n2:3\n$$\n\nTherefore, the liquid has a ratio of $2$ quarts of honey to $3$ quarts of water." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186525050 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186524939 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186525050 #edb/ref 1069 "q-6174" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186525050 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186525050 #edb/ref 1071 "Find the value of $0÷100.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186525050 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "df0053ff-176e-53fc-ba84-2cd6292eecda" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186525050 #edb/ref 1074 "Zero divided by any number (other than zero) equals zero. Therefore, $0÷100=0.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186525072 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186525127 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186525072 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "0b06fe31-a4e6-591b-bde8-a03fb5494049" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186525072 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186524993 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186525073 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186525072 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186525073 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "14073ad5-1bc7-58a3-ba38-cf30e5ac0012" #edb/ref 17592186045417